<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chester-le-Street</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chester-le-Street is a town in County Durham in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the site of a Roman fort. From 883 to 995 it was the seat of the community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a>, which had left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> with the saint's relics to escape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids. There the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a> were given their Old English gloss, before the community moved on to Durham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chester-le-street.jpg" alt="Chester-le-Street" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stuart.renneberg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Chester_Burn_Railway_Viaduct,_Chester-le-Street.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
